research-wiki/frontier-cells\ASTIS-SW-PBPS-actual-finite-jump-recursion.json:838:        "mathtext": "We first recall the usual Bouncy Particle Sampler (BPS) for readers\nunfamiliar with the construction [5]. It is a\npiecewise-deterministic Markov process on phase space\n$(x,p)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ , where $p$ denotes velocity (equivalently,\nmomentum for unit mass). Its invariant distribution is\n$\\mu\\otimes\\varphi$ , where $\\varphi=\\mathcal{N}(0,I)$ . Between events, the\nparticle moves along a straight line with constant $p$ :\n\n$\\dot{x}_{t}=p_{t}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\dot{p}_{t}=0\\,.$\n\n(2.1)\n\nAt state $(x,p)$ , a bounce occurs at rate\n\n$\\lambda_{\\rm BPS}(x,p)\\coloneqq[p^{\\top}\\nabla V(x)]_{+}\\,.$\n\n(2.2)\n\nEquivalently, conditional on the current state and in the absence of a\nvelocity refresh, the next bounce time $\\tau_{\\rm b}$ satisfies\n\n$\\mathbb{P}(\\tau_{\\rm b}>t\\mid x,p)=\\exp\\Bigl\\{-\\int_{0}^{t}[p^{\\top}\\nabla V(x+sp)]_{+}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\Bigr\\}\\,.$\n\n(2.3)\n\nAt a bounce, the position is unchanged and the velocity is specularly\nreflected against the gradient. For non-zero $h\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ , set\n\n$R_{h}\\coloneqq I-2\\,\\frac{hh^{\\top}}{\\|h\\|^{2}}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptR_{0}\\coloneqq I\\,.$\n\n(2.4)\n\nThe bounce update is $p\\leftarrow R_{\\nabla V(x)}p$ . It preserves\n$\\|p\\|$ , leaves the component tangent to a level set unchanged, and\nreverses the normal component, since\n$(R_{h}p)^{\\top}h=-p^{\\top}h$ . Thus, the particle travels freely\nwhile moving down the potential and bounces, at a rate equal to its\ninstantaneous uphill directional derivative, when it moves up the\npotential. Proximal BPS reuses this bounce mechanism, applied to a localized\nconditional target; the construction is given in Section 3.\n",
research-wiki/frontier-cells\ASTIS-SW-PBPS-actual-finite-jump-recursion.json:3060:        "mathtext": "Idea of the proof.\n\nThe identity (A.3) is a\nmomentum-reversed form of detailed balance. To see the mechanism\nformally, write\n$\\mathsf{L}_{y,\\widetilde{x}}=\\mathcal{A}+\\mathcal{J}$ , where\n\n$\\displaystyle\\mathcal{A}f(x,p)$\n$\\displaystyle\\coloneqq\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,p^{\\top}\\nabla_{x}f(x,p)-\\frac{(x-c_{y,\\widetilde{x}})^{\\top}}{\\sqrt{\\eta}}\\nabla_{p}f(x,p),$\n\n$\\displaystyle\\mathcal{J}f(x,p)$\n$\\displaystyle\\coloneqq\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(x,p)\\bigl\\{f(\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}(x,p))-f(x,p)\\bigr\\}.$\n\nMomentum reversal reverses the harmonic flow, so\n$\\mathsf{R}\\mathcal{A}\\mathsf{R}=-\\mathcal{A}$ . Moreover,\n\n$\\mathcal{A}\\log\\nu_{\\eta,y}(x,p)=-\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,p^{\\top}h_{\\widetilde{x}}(x)=-\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(x,p)+\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(x,-p).$\n\nFormal integration by parts gives\n\n$\\mathcal{A}^{*}=-\\mathcal{A}+\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}-\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}\\circ\\mathsf{R}.$\n\nThe bounce map $\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}$ is a\n$\\nu_{\\eta,y}$ -preserving involution, commutes with $\\mathsf{R}$ , and\nsatisfies $\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}\\circ\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}=\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}\\circ\\mathsf{R}$ . A formal change of variables\ntherefore gives\n\n$\\mathcal{J}^{*}G=(\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}\\circ\\mathsf{R})(G\\circ\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}})-\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}G.$\n\nCombining the two identities yields\n\n$\\mathsf{L}_{y,\\widetilde{x}}^{*}G=-\\mathcal{A}G+(\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}\\circ\\mathsf{R})(G\\circ\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}-G)=(\\mathsf{R}\\mathsf{L}_{y,\\widetilde{x}}\\mathsf{R})G.$\n\nFormally exponentiating this identity gives\n$(\\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}})^{*}=\\mathsf{R}\\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}\\mathsf{R}$ ; Gaussian momentum averaging\nthen removes $\\mathsf{R}$ and produces an ordinary self-adjoint position\nkernel.\n\nThis generator calculation is only heuristic as stated, as the standard arguments are not\nimmediately applicable because\n$\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}$ may be discontinuous where\n$h_{\\widetilde{x}}(x)=0$ . The pathwise argument below proves the same\nidentity without requiring differentiability or continuity of the\nbounce map.\n\nProof.\n\nAlong\n$\\zeta_{s}=(x_{s},p_{s})=\\Phi_{s}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}(\\zeta_{0})$ ,\n\n$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}s}\\log\\nu_{\\eta,y}(\\zeta_{s})=-\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,p_{s}^{\\top}h_{\\widetilde{x}}(x_{s})=-\\bigl\\{\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(\\zeta_{s})-\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(\\mathsf{R}\\zeta_{s})\\bigr\\}\\,.$\n\n(A.4)\n\nMoreover, by definition of $\\mathsf{R}$ and $\\Phi_{t}$ ,\n\n$\\mathsf{R}\\Phi_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}\\mathsf{R}=\\Phi_{-t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\mathsf{R}\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}=\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}\\mathsf{R}\\,.$\n\nIf $\\zeta^{+}=\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}\\zeta^{-}$ is a non-trivial jump, then\n$h_{\\widetilde{x}}(x)\\neq 0$ ,\n$(p^{-})^{\\top}h_{\\widetilde{x}}(x)>0$ , and\n\n$\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(\\zeta^{-})=\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(\\mathsf{R}\\zeta^{+})\\,.$\n\n(A.5)\n\nThe flow and $\\mathsf{R}$ preserve Lebesgue measure, as does the possibly\ndiscontinuous bounce map: by Fubini’s theorem and orthogonality, for\nevery integrable $F$ ,\n\n$\\int F(\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}(x,p))\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}p=\\int F(x,p)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}p\\,.$\n\n(A.6)\n\nTo pass from these identities to complete trajectories, let\n\n$\\Delta_{n}(t)\\coloneqq\\{(t_{1},\\ldots,t_{n}):0<t_{1}<\\cdots<t_{n}<t\\}\\,.$\n\nGiven $\\zeta_{0}$ and $\\mathbf{t}\\in\\Delta_{n}(t)$ , let\n$\\zeta_{s}^{\\zeta_{0},\\mathbf{t}}$ be the path obtained by following\n$\\Phi^{y,\\widetilde{x}}$ and applying $\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}$ at the\nprescribed times. Iterating the exponential clock construction gives,\nfor every bounded measurable functional $\\Psi$ of the path up to time\n$t$ ,\n\n$\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}_{\\zeta_{0}}\\Psi(\\zeta_{[0,t]})$\n$\\displaystyle=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\int_{\\Delta_{n}(t)}\\Psi(\\zeta_{[0,t]}^{\\zeta_{0},\\mathbf{t}})\\exp\\Bigl\\{-\\!\\int_{0}^{t}\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(\\zeta_{s}^{\\zeta_{0},\\mathbf{t}})\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\Bigr\\}\\prod_{j=1}^{n}\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(\\zeta_{t_{j}-}^{\\zeta_{0},\\mathbf{t}})\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{t}\\,.$\n\n(A.7)\n\nThe series is absolutely convergent because\n$\\lvert\\Delta_{n}(t)\\rvert=t^{n}/n!$ and the rate\nis bounded by $\\bar{\\lambda}_{\\mathcal{E}}(y,\\widetilde{x})$ .\nIntegrate (A.7) against\n$\\nu_{\\eta,y}(\\mathrm{d}\\zeta_{0})$ and apply\n\n$(\\zeta_{s})_{0\\leq s\\leq t}\\longmapsto(\\mathsf{R}\\zeta_{t-s})_{0\\leq s\\leq t}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt(t_{1},\\ldots,t_{n})\\longmapsto(t-t_{n},\\ldots,t-t_{1})\\,.$\n\n(A.8)\n\nFor completeness, we justify this change of variables despite the\npossible discontinuity of the bounce map. For fixed\n$\\mathbf{t}\\in\\Delta_{n}(t)$ , the endpoint map\n$\\zeta_{0}\\mapsto\\zeta_{t}^{\\zeta_{0},\\mathbf{t}}$ is a finite composition\nof the Borel involution $\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}$ and the affine\nharmonic flows $\\Phi_{u}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}$ . By\n(A.6) and the explicit flow formula, each factor is\na measure-preserving Borel bijection. Hence the endpoint map, and\ntherefore\n$\\zeta_{0}\\mapsto\\mathsf{R}\\zeta_{t}^{\\zeta_{0},\\mathbf{t}}$ , is a Borel\nmeasure-preserving bijection. The identities preceding\n(A.7) show that its inverse is obtained by\nusing the reversed ordered times in (A.8). The\nmap on $\\Delta_{n}(t)$ is linear with absolute determinant one.\nThus the full change of variables is measure preserving; no Jacobian is needed.\n\nIntegrating (A.4) over the deterministic arcs\nand using that a reflection preserves $\\nu_{\\eta,y}$ gives\n\n$\\nu_{\\eta,y}(\\zeta_{0})\\,e^{-\\int_{0}^{t}\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(\\zeta_{s})\\,\\mathrm{d}s}=\\nu_{\\eta,y}(\\zeta_{t})\\,e^{-\\int_{0}^{t}\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(\\mathsf{R}\\zeta_{s})\\,\\mathrm{d}s}\\,.$\n\nThis identity matches the initial density and survival factor of a\nforward path with those of its reversal, while\n(A.5) matches every jump-rate factor. Hence,\neach $n$ -jump term in (A.7) is unchanged by\n(A.8). Tonelli’s theorem justifies the termwise\nchange of variables for non-negative bounded functions; decomposing\nsigned functions into positive and negative parts then gives, for\nbounded measurable $F,G$ ,\n\n$\\int F\\,\\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}G\\,\\mathrm{d}\\nu_{\\eta,y}=\\int(\\mathsf{R}G)\\,\\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}(\\mathsf{R}F)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\nu_{\\eta,y}\\,.$\n\nTaking $F=1$ proves invariance, and the identity extends by density to\n(A.3) on $L^{2}(\\nu_{\\eta,y})$ .\n\nInvariance and Jensen’s inequality give\n$\\|\\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}F\\|_{2}\\leq\\|F\\|_{2}$ . It remains to prove\nstrong continuity. For $F\\in C_{\\rm c}(\\mathbb{R}^{2d})$ , the deterministic flow\nconverges to the identity as $t\\searrow 0$ , and the probability of a jump before time\n$t$ is\n\n$1-\\exp\\Bigl\\{-\\int_{0}^{t}\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(\\Phi_{s}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}\\zeta)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\Bigr\\}\\longrightarrow 0\\,.$\n\nDominated convergence therefore gives\n$\\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}F\\to F$ in $L^{2}$ . Density of\n$C_{\\rm c}(\\mathbb{R}^{2d})$ and contractivity extend this to all $F\\in L^{2}$ .\n\nFinally, Gaussian momentum averaging satisfies\n$\\mathsf{R}\\mathsf{\\Pi}_{p}^{*}=\\mathsf{\\Pi}_{p}^{*}$ and\n$\\mathsf{\\Pi}_{p}\\mathsf{R}=\\mathsf{\\Pi}_{p}$ . Combining these identities with\n(A.3) gives\n\n$(\\mathsf{\\Pi}_{p}\\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}\\mathsf{\\Pi}_{p}^{*})^{*}=\\mathsf{\\Pi}_{p}(\\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}})^{*}\\mathsf{\\Pi}_{p}^{*}=\\mathsf{\\Pi}_{p}\\mathsf{R}\\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}\\mathsf{R}\\mathsf{\\Pi}_{p}^{*}=\\mathsf{\\Pi}_{p}\\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}\\mathsf{\\Pi}_{p}^{*}\\,.$\n\nThis proves self-adjointness; the Markov and contraction properties are\ninherited from $\\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}$ .\n∎\n",
research-wiki/frontier-cells\ASTIS-SW-PBPS-actual-finite-jump-recursion.json:3307:        "mathtext": "To pass from these identities to complete trajectories, let\n\n$\\Delta_{n}(t)\\coloneqq\\{(t_{1},\\ldots,t_{n}):0<t_{1}<\\cdots<t_{n}<t\\}\\,.$\n\nGiven $\\zeta_{0}$ and $\\mathbf{t}\\in\\Delta_{n}(t)$ , let\n$\\zeta_{s}^{\\zeta_{0},\\mathbf{t}}$ be the path obtained by following\n$\\Phi^{y,\\widetilde{x}}$ and applying $\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}$ at the\nprescribed times. Iterating the exponential clock construction gives,\nfor every bounded measurable functional $\\Psi$ of the path up to time\n$t$ ,\n\n$\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}_{\\zeta_{0}}\\Psi(\\zeta_{[0,t]})$\n$\\displaystyle=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\int_{\\Delta_{n}(t)}\\Psi(\\zeta_{[0,t]}^{\\zeta_{0},\\mathbf{t}})\\exp\\Bigl\\{-\\!\\int_{0}^{t}\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(\\zeta_{s}^{\\zeta_{0},\\mathbf{t}})\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\Bigr\\}\\prod_{j=1}^{n}\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(\\zeta_{t_{j}-}^{\\zeta_{0},\\mathbf{t}})\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{t}\\,.$\n\n(A.7)\n\nThe series is absolutely convergent because\n$\\lvert\\Delta_{n}(t)\\rvert=t^{n}/n!$ and the rate\nis bounded by $\\bar{\\lambda}_{\\mathcal{E}}(y,\\widetilde{x})$ .\nIntegrate (A.7) against\n$\\nu_{\\eta,y}(\\mathrm{d}\\zeta_{0})$ and apply\n\n$(\\zeta_{s})_{0\\leq s\\leq t}\\longmapsto(\\mathsf{R}\\zeta_{t-s})_{0\\leq s\\leq t}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt(t_{1},\\ldots,t_{n})\\longmapsto(t-t_{n},\\ldots,t-t_{1})\\,.$\n\n(A.8)\n\nFor completeness, we justify this change of variables despite the\npossible discontinuity of the bounce map. For fixed\n$\\mathbf{t}\\in\\Delta_{n}(t)$ , the endpoint map\n$\\zeta_{0}\\mapsto\\zeta_{t}^{\\zeta_{0},\\mathbf{t}}$ is a finite composition\nof the Borel involution $\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}$ and the affine\nharmonic flows $\\Phi_{u}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}$ . By\n(A.6) and the explicit flow formula, each factor is\na measure-preserving Borel bijection. Hence the endpoint map, and\ntherefore\n$\\zeta_{0}\\mapsto\\mathsf{R}\\zeta_{t}^{\\zeta_{0},\\mathbf{t}}$ , is a Borel\nmeasure-preserving bijection. The identities preceding\n(A.7) show that its inverse is obtained by\nusing the reversed ordered times in (A.8). The\nmap on $\\Delta_{n}(t)$ is linear with absolute determinant one.\nThus the full change of variables is measure preserving; no Jacobian is needed.\n",
